add(96,'MEDIUM',
'''A workload appears lightly utilized, and the exercise asks whether a smaller instance is justified. Rightsizing must preserve performance and capacity requirements, including peak and changing demand.''',
'''Compare the supplied load, utilization and performance baseline with service constraints. Suppose one low-utilization sample suggests spare capacity, but the supplied peak period reaches the required performance boundary. Do not use the low sample alone to justify a smaller instance. A recommendation needs evidence that the proposed size still satisfies the supplied constraints across the relevant baseline. Record the workload requirement, observed baseline and reason to recommend or withhold a reduction. The objective is matching resource type and size to workload needs, not selecting the smallest available instance or inventing a price comparison.''',
'''Low utilization at one instant is not sufficient rightsizing evidence. Account for peak and changing demand and retain service constraints in the recommendation. Smaller is appropriate only when the supplied performance and capacity requirements remain satisfied.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f2,f3', 'Use supplied baseline and constraints; do not introduce EC2 pricing estimates or unsupported instance recommendations.')
add(97,'MEDIUM',
'''A security finding needs an accountable responder and a usable evidence handoff. Route the finding's actual context rather than replacing it with a stronger claim than the evidence supports.''',
'''Include the supplied finding ID or evidence link, affected resource, observed activity and urgency in the handoff to the responsible owner. Use the established escalation path. Preserve the evidence so the receiving responder can inspect the finding. If the report describes suspicious activity, retain that description rather than declaring the resource compromised without support.''',
'''A concise handoff should not erase evidence or exaggerate it. Resource identity, severity and activity details give responder context; an unsupported compromise claim does not replace the finding record.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f3', 'Use established owner and escalation path; do not author specialist security conclusions.')
add(98,'HIGH',
'''An operational event should reach one approved notification or established Systems Manager action. Pair the tested selection pattern with only the approved target, parameters and permissions.''',
'''Set the supplied event bus and event pattern, then add the approved SNS or Systems Manager target with its required parameters. Configure the target's required authorization model. Send the matching and nonmatching test events and inspect execution or notification evidence at the approved target. Compare the results with the intended rule selection. The setup task is not complete merely because the pattern matches in isolation; the approved target's evidence verifies the route.''',
'''A matching pattern is only the selection half of the rule. The approved target, parameters and required permissions are also needed, and positive/negative event tests should be checked against actual target evidence.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Use established Systems Manager action or approved SNS target; do not author a new response document.')
add(99,'HIGH',
'''An ECS service should keep a requested number of tasks running. The controlled task-removal test checks service reconciliation, not just the lifecycle of the removed task.''',
'''Create the service with the supplied cluster, task definition, network settings and desiredCount. During the exercise, remove the one approved task and inspect service events plus running and pending counts until the requested count is restored. For an example desired count of three, the record should show the controlled loss and restoration to that requested count. The service replaces stopped or failed tasks; the task definition supplies what runs, while the service maintains the desired number.''',
'''The removed task's final state is not the service's desired-state outcome. Inspect replacement behavior, service events and running/pending counts until desiredCount is restored.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f3', 'Use supplied removal procedure; defer task identity and rolling deployment details to their own concepts.')
add(100,'HIGH',
'''A promotion job must require both supplied unit and integration tests. Make failure visible to the workflow and preserve the dependency on both jobs rather than allowing promotion to ignore a failing test.''',
'''Run the supplied TEST_COMMAND values in test steps and set the promotion job's needs to the supplied unit and integration job names. Do not set continue-on-error on blocking test steps. Use the seeded failing test and inspect its nonzero exit result and the dependent promotion outcome. Record that the test failure prevents promotion; an independently successful other test is not enough to satisfy both gates.''',
'''A command that failed must not be converted into a tolerated blocking-test result with continue-on-error. The promotion dependency requires both test jobs, and the supplied failure case demonstrates that progression is blocked.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Use supplied tests; do not teach application test authoring or unfamiliar shell exit-handling syntax.')
add(101,'MEDIUM',
'''A small infrastructure change should be understandable at review and traceable through approved execution. Keep intent, focused diff, saved plan and resource verification in one change record.''',
'''Stage only the focused supplied files, commit with a descriptive supplied message and push the review branch through the established Git workflow. Open its supplied pull-request interface to request review. Include the change's intent, coherent diff and saved plan evidence for the affected EC2 resource. After supplied approval, run the supplied CI apply with that approved saved plan and attach execution plus resource-verification evidence. The example carries one focused change from review to verified outcome instead of mixing unrelated modifications into the submission.''',
'''Review approval alone is not the execution record. Retain the approved saved plan, supplied CI apply outcome and live-resource verification. A small coherent diff improves reviewability but still needs those execution checks.''', 'f1,f2,f3,f4','f3,f4', 'Require external Git/CI preparation; use supplied review interface and approved apply workflow.')
add(102,'HIGH',
'''A workload has capacity in more than one Availability Zone, but the controlled test must show required client-visible behavior during an approved AZ-component loss. Surviving resources alone do not establish availability.''',
'''Define the approved AZ-scoped failure and abort criteria before the experiment. During the supplied failure, monitor the client-visible service behavior. Compare the remaining independent capacity and request path with the service requirement. Suppose some instances survive but clients cannot complete the required request: record that the availability criterion was not demonstrated, rather than calling the test passed from the survivor count. Keep the failure scope, observed client behavior and remaining-path evidence together. This is deliberate resilience verification within supplied limits, not an exercise to disable an entire Region or infer availability from the existence of multiple locations.''',
'''A surviving instance count is not a client-visible availability result. Verify that remaining independent capacity and the request path maintain the required behavior, and keep the experiment within the approved AZ components and abort limits.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Use approved failure procedure; do not introduce regional failover or unsafe production faults.')
add(103,'HIGH',
'''Exercise teardown should remove only approved resources in dependency order. The operator also needs to inspect what was retained or failed to delete, without claiming deletion completion is billing verification.''',
'''Identify exercise resources by approved stack membership or tags. Remove dependents before their prerequisites and verify deletion results. For the supplied S3 bucket, empty objects, all object versions and delete markers before deleting the bucket; a current listing alone is not the stated version cleanup. Inspect any retained resources and failed deletions for approved follow-up. CloudFormation uses resource relationships during stack deletion, but that does not remove the need to inspect retained or failed resources. Keep the scope and per-resource deletion evidence in the cleanup record. A completed deletion operation is not a proof of zero charge: this task verifies resource removal, while the later billing check verifies residual charges.''',
'''Deleting a versioned bucket requires emptying versions and delete markers as well as objects. Also, a completed deletion operation is not billing verification. Inspect retained resources and deletion failures rather than assuming every exercise resource disappeared.''', 'f1,f2,f3,f4','f3,f4', 'Use approved scope and dependency order; leave residual-charge verification to its later concept.')
add(104,'MEDIUM',
'''Recovery rehearsal needs a valid session that can perform the required work. Check lifetime and authorization independently before using it for restoration.''',
'''Establish the approved recovery session and inspect its identity and expiry. Test the supplied backup-read and recovery-resource operations. Record both outcomes alongside the credential lifetime. If the credentials are unexpired but the backup-read test is denied, they are not ready for the required recovery. Conversely, permission evidence does not remove the need for a valid session during execution.''',
'''Unexpired credentials do not prove sufficient authorization. Recovery readiness needs both valid lifetime and successful required access tests for backup reading and resource creation.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f3', 'Use approved session provider and safe supplied tests; do not expose credentials.')
add(105,'HIGH',
'''A recovery rehearsal must stay inside the approved environment. Confirm restoration destinations and test traffic before provisioning resources, not only after clients begin using them.''',
'''Prepare the supplied isolated account and network target. Inspect restoration destinations and client test traffic settings to confirm they do not point to production. Record approved scope and stop conditions before provisioning or redirecting traffic. Keep those settings beside the rehearsal record so isolation is evidence of the actual targets, not just a label on the exercise.''',
'''Calling an account a sandbox is not the complete isolation check. Restoration destinations and client traffic must also stay out of production, and approved scope/stop conditions must be recorded before the experiment acts.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Use supplied isolated target; do not create production cutover or new network-isolation design.')
add(106,'HIGH',
'''A protected Automation operation requires approval and an enforceable stop path. Position that decision before the operation and inspect the actual execution state when stopping.''',
'''Place the supplied aws:approve step before the protected action. Use the established branching or failure path so rejection or stop prevents that operation. In the supplied stop-condition test, use the approved stop operation and inspect execution state before allowing further actions. The approval step pauses for designated approvers; it is not authorization to run the protected action before they decide.''',
'''An approval request is not an approval outcome. Rejection or stop must prevent the protected operation, and a stop request still requires inspecting execution state before further action is allowed.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Use supplied branching and stop operation; do not invent Automation document syntax.')
add(107,'MEDIUM',
'''An operational response needs an audit record for successful and failed steps. Connect the Automation execution context with CloudTrail evidence of the underlying AWS actions.''',
'''Record the execution ID, acting role, targets, start/end times and per-step outcomes from the supplied response. Inspect inputs, outputs and failure information as part of that record. Use CloudTrail request identity and operation evidence to attribute the AWS actions. If a step failed, retain its evidence rather than omitting it from a success-focused summary. This makes the record describe what the response actually attempted and achieved.''',
'''Audit evidence is not only a list of successful steps. Inputs, outputs, failure information and attributed underlying operations all matter. Keep both success and failure outcomes with the execution identity and targets.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f3', 'Use supplied execution/CloudTrail records; do not infer actions from the operator’s intended plan.')
add(108,'HIGH',
'''Audit logs need tamper evidence as well as storage controls. CloudTrail digest validation checks the delivered log interval; it does not itself prevent someone from accessing or deleting the files.''',
'''Substitute the supplied trail ARN, start/end times and Region into aws cloudtrail validate-logs. For an organization trail, include the required --account-id for the supplied account. Inspect validation failures for that log/digest interval. The validation uses signed digest files and hashes to detect modification or deletion of delivered logs. Keep the interval and validation result in the evidence record rather than declaring all logs intact from an unrelated successful interval. A passing validation result is tamper-evidence verification for the checked material, not an authorization control or prevention of future deletion.''',
'''Digest validation detects tampering; it does not prevent unauthorized access or deletion. Run it on the supplied log/digest interval and preserve failure evidence, keeping that role distinct from access and retention controls.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f3', 'Include organization-trail account-id where required; do not invent validation output or claim preventive protection.')
add(109,'MEDIUM',
'''Audit retention applies to individual object versions, and the selected Object Lock mode changes what bypass is possible. Verify version retention rather than inspecting only the bucket default.''',
'''On the prepared Object Lock bucket, apply the supplied default duration and mode. Inspect audit-object version retention and its retain-until date. For compliance mode, deletion or shortening is prevented during that period even by root. For governance mode, bypass requires the necessary bypass authorization and request. Record the mode and version-specific retention rather than describing all Object Lock retention as identical.''',
'''Governance and compliance modes have different bypass boundaries. Governance permits authorized requested bypass; compliance does not permit deletion or shortened retention during the period even by root. Check the individual audit-object version.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f3', 'Use prepared bucket and supplied retention policy; do not create irreversible unapproved compliance settings.')
add(110,'HIGH',
'''Terraform's remote state bucket must already exist before it can store state for the configuration that manages exercise resources. Keep backend bootstrap, backend selection and migration distinct.''',
'''Provision the protected backend bucket with the supplied local-state bootstrap configuration. Substitute the actual supplied bucket, key and Region in the line-separated backend block. The block configures state storage; it is not the resource declaration that creates the bucket. After changing the backend, run terraform init -migrate-state, approve the supplied migration and verify state at the intended bucket/key. Keep the bootstrap result and migrated-state location with the record so the workflow does not assume a backend can create itself before initialization. Apply the supplied protection settings without introducing a new backend design or another storage service.''',
'''Backend configuration names an already-provisioned state location; it is separate from managed-resource declarations. Provision the backend bucket first, then initialize/migrate and verify the intended bucket/key rather than treating the HCL block as bucket creation.''', 'f1,f2,f3,f4','f1,f2,f4', 'Show line-separated HCL, not comma-separated shorthand; use supplied bootstrap and migrate-state approval.')
add(111,'HIGH',
'''A replacement plan can have a permitted order or be prevented by a lifecycle rule. These controls affect mechanics, but the operator still needs scope and impact approval for a permitted change.''',
'''Set the supplied lifecycle argument in the resource block and introduce the approved controlled replacement-triggering change. Inspect the plan's replacement order or prevention error. By default Terraform destroys the old object before creating its replacement; create_before_destroy requests the reverse where supported. prevent_destroy rejects a destruction plan while that rule remains in configuration. If the plan is permitted, follow the supplied approval before applying it. Record the order or rejection evidence separately from scope/impact authorization. Do not describe a lifecycle setting as a substitute for review or as a guarantee that every replacement can be performed with both objects coexisting.''',
'''create_before_destroy is a request for reversed replacement order where supported, not a universal feasibility guarantee. prevent_destroy applies while its rule remains in configuration. Neither lifecycle setting replaces scope and impact approval.''', 'f1,f2,f3,f4','f1,f2,f4', 'Preserve where-supported and rule-remains qualifiers; do not imply protection after configuration removal.')
add(112,'HIGH',
'''An isolated recovery target needs replacement EC2 infrastructure from a supplied CloudFormation template. Confirm authority, Region and parameters before creation, then verify recovery readiness beyond stack completion.''',
'''Use the approved recovery credentials, explicit isolated account/Region target and required parameters with the supplied restoration template. Monitor stack events until creation completes. Inspect resource IDs and outputs, then run the supplied instance and service recovery-ready checks. If the stack completed but the service check fails, record those separate outcomes. The template provisions declared resources under the target account's execution authority and selected Region; the later checks establish whether the created instance and service are recovery-ready. This exercise does not switch production endpoints or introduce an independently designed restoration template.''',
'''Stack creation completion is not the whole recovery-readiness result. Resource IDs/outputs and supplied instance/service checks must be inspected. Keep the explicit isolated target and approved execution authority with the creation evidence.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f3', 'Use supplied template and checks; do not introduce production cutover or guest-OS authoring.')
add(113,'HIGH',
'''A release security scan is useful as a gate only when its evidence is compared with an explicit severity threshold and exception policy. The result should control promotion according to that policy.''',
'''Read the supplied Inspector report's affected resource, vulnerability evidence and severity. Compare findings with the stated threshold and documented exceptions. For an actionable unexcepted finding, make the supplied policy check return failure and require success before promotion. Retain the report and the policy decision. If a documented exception applies, record that policy basis rather than silently dropping the finding. This is a bounded release decision using supplied scan evidence, not a lesson in vulnerability exploitation, specialist remediation or inventing a new severity policy.''',
'''A scan report alone is not an enforced release gate. The policy check must fail for actionable unexcepted findings and promotion must require its success. Also, severity should be read alongside the documented threshold and exception policy.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Use supplied scan/threshold/exceptions; do not invent vulnerability facts or policy exceptions.')
add(114,'HIGH',
'''A pipeline has stopped, and several later actions did not run. Start with the first failing action in the selected execution instead of treating every unrun dependent action as an independent failure.''',
'''Open the supplied execution and action history. Identify the first failing action, inspect its input artifact revision and follow its linked service logs. Use the error details to select the approved correction. Retest and verify that action succeeds before investigating later dependent actions that did not run. Keep execution and revision identities with the evidence so a successful retry of a different input is not mistaken for correcting this failure. The example follows the pipeline's dependency break and service evidence; it does not guess the cause from the name of a later blocked stage.''',
'''Not run is not the same evidence as failed with an error. Find the first failing action and its input/logs, retest the approved correction and verify success before investigating dependent actions that could not progress.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Use supplied logs/error evidence; do not invent corrective commands or unsupported causes.')
add(115,'HIGH',
'''Telemetry shows a workload symptom, but the cause may be in the host, instance software or container. Combine the supplied time window with component inspection before changing anything.''',
'''Use the supplied metric/log interval to locate the affected workload. For EC2, read system status checks as underlying AWS-host evidence and instance checks as instance software/networking evidence. Inspect component service status, events and logs using the prepared Linux skills and supplied procedure. For a container, inspect its state, exit status and recent logs. Select the approved correction supported by that evidence and repeat the supplied health check. If a metric improved but the required health check still fails, retain that outcome rather than declaring recovery from a single signal. The symptom identifies what to investigate; it does not by itself prove the cause.''',
'''System and instance status checks concern different boundaries. A metric or log symptom is not automatically its own cause. Inspect the component and repeat the supplied health check after an evidence-supported correction.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f2,f3', 'Require external Linux service/process preparation; do not author new inspection commands or boot-startup configuration.')
add(116,'HIGH',
'''Deployment completion must be followed by a live smoke check for the intended revision. The validation action should report success to CodePipeline only after the supplied behavior check passes.''',
'''Run the supplied smoke-check action against the deployed endpoint and compare its response with the expected response for that revision. In the supplied Lambda validation action, send PutJobSuccessResult with the job ID only for a passing check. For failure, send PutJobFailureResult so dependent progression remains blocked. Keep the revision, endpoint result and reported job outcome in the evidence.''',
'''A completed deployment operation is not a live application check. The smoke test validates behavior, and its passing result must precede PutJobSuccessResult. Report failure rather than advancing dependent progression when the supplied check fails.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f3', 'Use supplied smoke action/Lambda scaffolding; do not teach application endpoint test authoring.')
add(117,'HIGH',
'''An event reaches a target that cannot accept it. Configure a finite retry budget and failure capture so the supplied delivery failure can be inspected instead of disappearing into indefinite retries.''',
'''Set RetryPolicy MaximumRetryAttempts and MaximumEventAgeInSeconds to the approved finite limits. Configure DeadLetterConfig with the approved SQS queue ARN and required queue permissions. Inspect the seeded exhausted-delivery failure in that queue and retain its evidence. Retryable target-delivery failures are retried within both age and attempt limits. Some nonretryable failures go directly to capture, so do not expect every failure to exhibit the full retry sequence. The example verifies the bounded policy and captured failure together; merely configuring a retry count does not prove that the capture queue can accept or holds the supplied failed event.''',
'''Retry configuration does not guarantee that every failure receives retries. Some nonretryable failures go directly to capture. Also, a configured dead-letter queue requires the required permissions and inspection of the seeded failure to verify capture.''', 'f1,f2,f3,f4','f3,f4', 'Use supplied finite limits and SQS queue; do not invent retry intervals or guaranteed retry causes.')
add(118,'HIGH',
'''An ECS service needs a new image revision without treating the update request as proof of a healthy rollout. Read the supplied replacement bounds and observe service/task health until steady-state criteria pass.''',
'''Register the supplied new image in a task-definition revision and update the service to it. Inspect service events, running task revisions and health against the supplied criteria. minimumHealthyPercent and maximumPercent describe replacement bounds relative to desiredCount. Keep those settings with the observations rather than assuming every rolling update replaces all tasks at once. Stop calling the rollout complete at the update request; verify the supplied steady state.''',
'''Service-update acceptance is not a healthy rollout result. Verify events, running revisions and health until the supplied steady-state criteria are met, using the configured percent bounds relative to desiredCount.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f3', 'Use supplied task definition and steady-state checks; do not add load-driven ECS scaling.')
add(119,'MEDIUM',
'''A staging-tested release should promote the same built content to the next environment. Change the supplied environment configuration without rebuilding the source into a different output.''',
'''Carry the same immutable artifact reference or digest from staging to production and supply production's approved configuration. Compare deployed digests in both environments and their recorded build provenance. If the digests differ, the evidence does not show that the tested output was promoted. Build produces the artifact, release combines it with environment configuration, and run executes that release.''',
'''Rebuilding from the same source is not this same-artifact promotion check. Promote the immutable reference/digest and verify deployed digests with build provenance; environment configuration belongs to the release combination.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f2,f3', 'Use supplied artifact and environment configuration; do not create a new build during promotion.')
add(120,'MEDIUM',
'''Teardown removed resources, but the bill still needs a time-aware check. Cost Explorer may report older usage after deletion, and an absence of resources is not a zero-cost result.''',
'''Filter the supplied teardown interval by account and service or usage type. Compare usage timestamps with resource-deletion evidence. A charge associated with usage before deletion is different from evidence of continuing usage after deletion. Record that timing distinction and repeat the check after the billing-data refresh. Cost Explorer refreshes at least once every 24 hours, and some data can take longer to appear. Do not declare zero cost solely because the resources are gone or the first post-teardown report is empty. This exercise verifies residual charges against deletion evidence and reporting delay rather than inventing a new price estimate.''',
'''A charge appearing after deletion is not automatically proof of post-deletion usage. Compare usage timestamps and repeat after data refresh. Conversely, resource absence or an early empty report does not prove zero cost.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f3', 'Preserve at-least-daily refresh and possible longer appearance delay; do not promise a fixed final billing time.')
add(121,'HIGH',
'''Remote Terraform state can contain sensitive values, so its reader/writer boundary needs its own access test. Backend access isolation is different from coordinating concurrent authorized writers.''',
'''Scope the supplied readers/writers to the required ListBucket prefix and GetObject/PutObject state-object operations. Configure supplied locking permissions separately. Initialize the intended backend and provision the separate exercise bucket through the approved identity. Test that the unapproved identity cannot read or overwrite state, recording those outcomes without exposing state content. The selected bucket/key access boundary protects sensitive state; locking serves a different coordination purpose.''',
'''Locking is not the state authorization boundary. State access isolation prevents unauthorized access, while a separate mechanism coordinates authorized writers. Test unapproved read and overwrite rather than assuming backend initialization proves isolation.''', 'f1,f2,f3,f4','f1,f4', 'Keep state content private; use supplied bucket/key/prefix permissions and approved/unapproved identities.')
add(122,'HIGH',
'''Two authorized Terraform operations must not change shared state at the same time. Enable the S3 lock-file mechanism and test contention without bypassing another active writer.''',
'''Set use_lockfile = true in the supplied backend and grant the supplied GetObject, PutObject and DeleteObject permissions on the .tflock object. During the controlled state-changing operation holding the lock, start the supplied competing operation. Verify it cannot acquire the lock or change state. Do not disable locking to make the test proceed. If a lock remains from a failed operation and automatic unlocking failed, force-unlock has a specific recovery role; it is not a safe bypass for another active writer. Keep this coordination check distinct from tests of unauthorized direct state-object access.''',
'''S3 lock-file locking is opt-in, not automatic for every backend. It coordinates concurrent writers and is not an authorization boundary. Force-unlock is reserved for a failed operation's leftover lock when automatic unlocking failed, not for bypassing an active writer.''', 'f1,f2,f3,f4,m1','f1,f4,m1', 'Use current S3 .tflock behavior; do not introduce obsolete mandatory DynamoDB locking or active-writer bypass.')
add(123,'HIGH',
'''A production promotion needs an authorized decision for the exact staged revision. The approval notification and the approver's authority are separate from the pipeline's wait point.''',
'''Place the supplied manual approval between staging and production with its review link or revision context and designated approver permissions. Verify the pipeline stops at that action. After approval of the intended execution and revision, verify it advances. Rejection or expiration fails the action. Receiving a notification does not itself grant permission to approve, so check the authorized approver rather than treating every recipient as eligible.''',
'''A notification is not approval authority, and approval of one revision is not the recorded approval of another. Verify the intended execution/revision stops and advances only after its authorized approval.''', 'f1,f2,f3,m1','f2,f3,m1', 'Use supplied approval context and permissions; do not invent expiration durations or approvers.')
add(124,'HIGH',
'''An incident matches an established runbook, but routine execution still depends on its preconditions and expected outcomes. Keep verification and stop decisions at each action boundary.''',
'''Check the supplied runbook's preconditions before taking the first approved action. Execute only the stated approved steps and compare each actual result with its expected operational outcome before advancing. If a supplied stop condition occurs or a result falls outside the runbook's bounds, stop routine execution and escalate with the observed evidence. Record the last completed action and the result that required escalation so the next responder need not guess what happened. This example applies a known procedure safely; it does not authorize improvising further resource changes because the initial diagnosis seemed familiar.''',
'''A known runbook is not unconditional permission to continue. Preconditions, expected results and stop bounds govern execution. An out-of-bounds result calls for stopping and escalation with evidence, not an unapproved extension of the procedure.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Use established runbook and owners; do not invent recovery actions or skip expected-result checks.')
add(125,'HIGH',
'''An isolated rehearsal restored a service, but the recovery objectives need two separate measured comparisons. Use the defined disruption start, readiness result and recovered data markers to test RTO and RPO.''',
'''For an illustrative rehearsal, suppose disruption starts at 10:00 and the supplied readiness checks pass at 10:18. Elapsed restoration time is 18 minutes; compare that with the stated RTO, not with the data marker. If the latest recovered marker is 09:56, the gap from the 10:00 disruption point is 4 minutes; compare that with the stated RPO. With example objectives of 20 minutes RTO and 5 minutes RPO, both comparisons pass. If the marker were 09:50 instead, the service could still pass the time comparison while failing the data-loss comparison. These numbers are illustrative measurements, not AWS defaults. Retain the timestamps, marker and readiness evidence for both conclusions.''',
'''A running service alone does not prove either objective was met. Measure elapsed time to passing readiness checks for RTO and the recovered-data gap for RPO. A good result for one cannot substitute for the other.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f1,f2,f3', 'Use isolated rehearsal and supplied readiness/data markers; do not substitute resource status for service checks.')
add(126,'MEDIUM',
'''A verified incident fix should lead to a factual timeline and one justified preventive action. Keep observations separate from causal conclusions so the follow-up does not overstate what the evidence established.''',
'''Write the timeline from the supplied timestamps and observations: symptom, investigation evidence, approved correction and verified result. State which causal conclusion the verified fix supports rather than treating every nearby event as a contributing cause. Record one preventive action tied to that demonstrated cause, name its accountable owner and state how completion will be checked. If the evidence supports only one narrow cause, keep the action equally bounded. The post-incident analysis should describe impact, events, contributing causes and recurrence-reduction work, not assign unsupported blame or add unrelated improvements.''',
'''A plausible causal story is not the same as a supported conclusion. Separate direct observations from conclusions backed by the verified fix, and give the justified preventive action an owner and verification method.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Use supplied evidence only; do not invent causal facts, blame or unrelated preventive work.')
add(127,'HIGH',
'''A restored target has passed its readiness checks, and clients now need to reach it through the approved endpoint change. Verify convergence rather than assuming every client immediately follows the new setting.''',
'''Confirm the restored target's supplied readiness checks before the approved DNS or application-endpoint switch. Perform that switch through the supplied procedure and test that clients reach the restored target. For a DNS switch, account for cached answers and TTL while checking client convergence. A changed DNS setting and an old client answer can coexist during caching; retain the actual client-target evidence instead of declaring all clients moved at the update request.''',
'''Endpoint configuration is not the same as verified client convergence. For DNS, cached answers and TTL matter; test clients reaching the ready restored target rather than assuming an immediate universal switch.''', 'f1,f2,f3','f2,f3', 'Use approved cutover procedure only after readiness; do not invent DNS record commands or TTL defaults.')
